I don't think anyone is unaware of the  grotesque and the growing gap between  those who own huge wealth and those who  live in poverty what I find frustrating  and very odd is that although we have  identified the fact that the richest 1%  own half the world's wealth and we have  identified the fact that 3.5 billion  people have assets of less than $10,000  we do not identify a further crucial  identifier which is that the richest  people in the world are men and the  poorest are women now that last report  came out in November last year there was  another report that came out in November  last year by the World Bank and UN women  and that had analyzed household survey  data for 89 countries by sex by age by  household composition and other  variables  and again it found that more women than  men live in poverty  it found that households with children  were among the poorest and that single  parents with children and predominantly  single mothers with children which are  the vast majority of single parents  faced a far higher risk of poverty so UN  Women stated that the policy  implications of its findings are very  clear that eradicating poverty was going  to require greater attention to the  vulnerabilities faced by women during  specific periods of their lives the  United Nations sustainable development  goal number one is ending poverty gender  equality is goal number five meanwhile  here in Britain a new report last week  identified that 14 million people are  now living in poverty that includes 4.5  million UK children  the survey was described in one  newspaper as the most sophisticated  analysis yet of material disadvantage in  the UK the most significant innovation  being to build core living costs such as  childcare into its poverty measure the  survey found that poverty is especially  prevalent in families with at least one  disabled person households where no one  works or that are dependent on income  for income on irregular or 0s jobs and  in single-parent families now this study  comes 10 years after austerity was first  introduced it comes one year after  research by the House of Commons library  showed that women were bearing 86% of  the austerity burden that study showed  that in cutting Social Security on which  women disproportionately rely because of  the societal pressures that push more  women than men into unpaid care rules in  addition with cutting investment in care  on which women disproportionately rely  for the same reason the government had  essentially funded its austerity program  by taking money out of poor women's  purses to create tax breaks for the rich  and the rich as we know are  disproportionately men so two things are  clear to me the first is that an  economic model that recognizes the lived  experience of women would tackle poverty  far better than existing ones and the  second thing this is very clear to me is  that not using the right models is not  the biggest problem here the biggest  problem is that there is a huge  reluctance to acknowledge that any  change in approach is needed after 10  years of austerity that hit women  disproportionately  why are we still not talking about the  fact that rising poverty levels and  inequality is deeply rooted in women's  inequality and that solving women's  inequality would make life better for  everyone with all the work that the  World Bank and the United Nations are  doing to tackle poverty and as it  repeatedly throws up data showing that  women experience it disproportionately  why are we still not prioritizing  women's equality as the route to ending  poverty before I did this job as a funny  sort of job I feels odd describing as a  job but before I did this job I used to  work for Reuters news agency and I  worked there for about 20 years and I  covered everything from stock markets to  currency markets to oil markets  economics to politics to trade  technology diplomacy Foreign Affairs I  did all sorts of stuff and the first  thing I noticed most quickly of all as a  rookie reporter although it was  subsequently borne out by years of  financial reporting and editing was how  quickly investors move when there's a  chance of making money on the next big  thing it basically all comes down to  fear and greed and all it would take  would be one report from one of the big  investment banks or the professional  services organizations and whoosh they'd  be off there'd be a stampede either the  rush at the rush to buy rubles  Horo those days the technology bubble at  the endless ups and downs of the oil  price the stampede to Brazil the  Stampede to China by stark contrast a  raft of analyses from Price Waterhouse  Cooper to Grant Thornton to McKinsey to  Barclays to the EIB all showing that  women's equality could add billions to  companies to countries GDP  and company's profits was met by deadly  silence tumbleweed we have ignored women  for so long that even when their  experience is shown to be at the heart  of the biggest problems confronting  modern society we will look away from an  effective resolution rather than look  directly at women we have to see women  and to do that we have to stop seeing  them as a special interest group a  subsection of real life a thinner  version of the male experience the  reason we set up the women's equality  party was to show first of all that  solving inequality requires women  specific policies and then also to show  that it is actually possible to design  for women in a way that benefits  everyone  that embracing difference benefits  everyone that exposing the flaws of  existing designs is necessary for the  advancement of all in society that  putting the furthest first because the  poorest and the most marginalized are  always women forces us to go beyond the  limitations of the current binary  socialism or conservatism that feminism  is a political ideology in and of itself  we have to challenge the idea that  feminism belongs to all the other  parties to claim as they see fit as a  secondary consideration to more  important things and in particular we  have to challenge ideas about identity  politics about whose identities we are  considering in current politics  now you see by that such a little ripple  that went through there really is  nothing so derided in the marketplace of  political ideas as identity politics it  is a term that immediately conjures a  cult of the humorless arbiters of  offense we shut down imaginative debate  in the name of esoteric special  interests this powerful narrative is  rooted even in the dictionary definition  of identity politics which goes a  tendency for people of a particular  religion race social background etc to  form exclusive political alliances  moving away from traditional broad-based  party politics and I think it's  astonishing that anyone still thinks  politics is broadly for everyone  particularly in the age of Trump and  Farage and their assault on the liberal  elite for more debates talks and  interviews subscribe today to the  institutes of arson ideas at IAI TV  you
 

